  "title": "Java’s Memory Strategy.",
  "body": " Java’s Memory Strategy   How does Java’s automatic garbage collection differ from manual memory management and compile-time ownership?      A background service in the JVM monitors the heap at runtime and automatically reclaims unused memory, trading a small amount of bookkeeping overhead for safety and simplicity.    Correct! Java’s runtime garbage collector frees unused heap memory automatically, eliminating memory leaks and dangling pointers while requiring modest CPU and memory overhead.      The Java compiler enforces strict ownership rules at build time and inserts cleanup instructions so no runtime service is needed.    Not quite. That describes compile-time ownership (as used in Rust), whereas Java manages heap memory at runtime.      The Java programmer must write explicit instructions to free every heap object when it is no longer needed.    Not quite. That describes manual memory management in C and C++; Java programmers do not manually free heap objects.      Java avoids using the heap entirely by storing all dynamic objects on the call stack.    Not quite. Java uses the heap extensively for dynamic data and objects, relying on the garbage collector to manage that heap space.     "
