  "number": "2.4.1.4.2",
  "title": "Statements vs. Expressions.",
  "body": " Statements vs. Expressions   In Java, what is the difference between a statement and an expression?      A statement is a complete instruction to do something, while an expression calculates a single value.    Correct! Statements tell the program what action to perform, and many statements contain expressions that Java evaluates down to a single value.      A statement always calculates a single value, while an expression must end with a semicolon.    Not quite. Simple statements end with a semicolon, while expressions calculate or represent a single value.      A statement can only contain literal values, while an expression can only contain variable names.    Not quite. Both literals and variables can be expressions, and statements can contain both.      A statement and an expression mean the same thing; Java uses both terms interchangeably.    Not quite. Statements are complete instructions, while expressions are pieces of code that evaluate to a value.     "
