  "title": "Evaluating Literals and Variables.",
  "body": " Evaluating Literals and Variables   Suppose depth currently holds 4 , and then the statement depth = depth \/ 12.0; executes. Afterward, what do the expression depth and the literal 12.0 evaluate to?       depth evaluates to 0.3333... (the last value assigned to it), while 12.0 evaluates to 12.0 .    Correct! A variable evaluates to the last value assigned to it during execution, while a literal always evaluates to what you literally typed.       depth evaluates to \"depth\" , while 12.0 evaluates to 4 .    Not quite. A variable never evaluates to its own name, and a literal evaluates to the value you typed.       depth evaluates to 4 , while 12.0 evaluates to 0.3333... .    Not quite. depth was updated by the assignment statement, and the literal 12.0 never changes.      Both depth and 12.0 evaluate to 4 .    Not quite. depth holds the new quotient after the assignment, and 12.0 evaluates to 12.0 .     "
